        "id": 1067053
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-12-16T14:19:12Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hi Mark, \n\nI can see a couple of issues with this:\n\nyou seem to lose the width of the access as you log it (HW usually *is* width-sensitive)\n\u003cPrasd\u003e I have not changed HW read/write operation and just logging after read and before write. \n\nthere is no way to select the scope of the tracing (it is very unlikely that you want to trace every single MMIO access in the system, and more likely that you\u0027d want a specific subsystem instead)\n\u003cPrasad\u003e Yes. You are right. We want every single MMIO access operation.\n\nthis creates a new ABI, which has to be agreed upon upstream first\n\u003cPrasad\u003e I have tried upstreaming and but didn\u0027t land into upstream.\n\nIf you are trying to track accesses to devices that are not clock, it feels like you should involve the clock subsystem directly, and track the dependency between a device and its clock provider, rather than having a blanket MMIO tracing.\n\u003cPrasad\u003e Hi Mark, Qualcomm have this use case to identify the unblocked access through logging. There is no other way SW developer can identify all register reads and writes to debug their drivers in regular development and testing phase.\n\nQualcomm uses this logging in the development phase and this tracevents are not enabled in commercial builds.\n\n\n-Thanks, Prasad\n",
      "revId": "1471381498f814403430dbcd3a912114e4a60c49",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
